e the unit criterion. Reindex a simultaneous lattice/complex basis by I using finite-dimensionality and cardinality; use the library's strong rank condition for the field.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R002.BG001\", \"R002.BG002\", \"R002.BG003\", \"R002.BG004\"]}, {\"id\": \"T007\", \"statement\": \"Nonempty (LR.Action K M rho x y h) ∧ ∃ f : LR.MR p M →ₗ[ℤ] V, LR.IotaSpec p M f ∧ LR.LocalBasis p l M f ∧ LR.Preserved p M f (rho x) (rho y)\", \"inputs\": [\"H002\", \"H004\", \"T006\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S007\"], \"explanation\": \"Base-change the simultaneous integer basis to R; construct the pure-tensor embedding and prove injectivity through complex independence. Extend lattice operators to the scalar extension, prove bracket closure of the integer span, and construct the induced K-linear action with its pure-tensor commutator law.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R002.BG005\", \"R002.BG006\"]}, {\"id\": \"T008\", \"statement\": \"LR.SmallUnits p l\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T006\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S008\"], \"explanation\": \"Apply the pinned factorial divisibility theorem at r≤l.toNat<p. Use the exact R fraction characterization to invert factorials and each binomial coefficient.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T009\", \"statement\": \"∀ b : Module.Basis (LR.I l) ℂ V, LR.BasisSpec l (rho y) v b → LR.UnitIdentities l (rho x) (rho y) b\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S009\"], \"explanation\": \"Check the three operator identities on every basis vector using the existing divided-power formulas; Fin.last l.toNat represents the source index λ.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R002.BG007\", \"R002.BG008\"]}, {\"id\": \"T010\", \"statement\": \"LR.N2 p l K M rho x y h v\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T006\", \"T007\", \"T008\", \"T009\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S010\"], \"explanation\": \"Combine the three identities and invert the binomial scalar to obtain each matrix unit. Closure under composition and R-scalars proves preservation of the embedded arbitrary lattice. Bundle all N002 clauses without assuming an embedding or action in advance.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T011\", \"statement\": \"∀ (r : LR.R p) (c : LR.C p), LR.cv p (r • c) = (((r : ℚ) : ℂ)) * LR.cv p c\", \"inputs\": [], \"source_steps\": [\"S011\"], \"explanation\": \"Checked representation bridge for the restriction-of-scalars wrapper: use the pinned RestrictScalars.smul_def and the subtype algebra map R→ℚ followed by the rational embedding ℚ→ℂ. cv is the supplied additive equivalence, so zero, sums and bijectivity already hold.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T012\", \"statement\": \"∀ f : LR.MR p M →ₗ[ℤ] V, LR.IotaSpec p M f → ∃ J : Submodule (LR.R p) (LR.C p), LR.CoeffSpec p l (rho y) v M f J\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T010\", \"T011\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S011\"], \"explanation\": \"The pure-tensor specification makes f the unique canonical embedding. Matrix units identify all coordinate modules with J0; projected finite basis coordinates generate J. Prove it nonzero and build the coordinate R-linear equivalence. cv makes the restriction-of-scalars identification explicit.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T013\", \"statement\": \"∀ J : Submodule (LR.R p) (LR.C p), J.FG → LR.RationalEmbedding p J\", \"inputs\": [\"T011\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S012\"], \"explanation\": \"Tensor a finite generating set to prove finite-dimensionality. Construct μ by multiplication; put any finite tensor sum over a common nonzero integer denominator to prove injectivity. Its range is exactly the rational span.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R003.BG001\", \"R003.BG002\", \"R003.BG003\", \"R003.BG004\", \"R003.BG005\", \"R003.BG006\"]}, {\"id\": \"T014\", \"statement\": \"Module.finrank ℚ (ℚ ⊗[LR.R p] LR.MR p M) = l.toNat+1\", \"inputs\": [\"T010\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S013\"], \"explanation\": \"Choose the R-basis exported by N002 and apply Basis.baseChange over ℚ, then finrank_eq_card_basis. No M premise port is required.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T015\", \"statement\": \"∀ (f : LR.MR p M →ₗ[ℤ] V) (J : Submodule (LR.R p) (LR.C p)), LR.IotaSpec p M f → LR.CoeffSpec p l (rho y) v M f J → Module.finrank ℚ (ℚ ⊗[LR.R p] J) = 1\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T013\", \"T014\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S014\"], \"explanation\": \"Tensor the coordinate equivalence with ℚ. Construct inverse coordinate maps to identify the result with I→(ℚ⊗R J). Use finite-dimen…235 tokens truncated…ions.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T018\", \"statement\": \"LR.N3 p l (rho y) v M\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T010\", \"T012\", \"T013\", \"T015\", \"T016\", \"T017\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S017\"], \"explanation\": \"Use a finite nonempty list of nonzero generators, rational multiples of a and their p-decompositions. A minimum valuation produces b and J=Rb. Prove the scaled basis property for every such nonzero generator; bundle the coordinate decomposition, μ and rational rank as N003.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T019\", \"statement\": \"Nonempty (LR.Residue p K)\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T010\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S018\"], \"explanation\": \"Construct the maps R→K and R→ZMod p by the fraction formula; prove compatibility with the prime-field map, surjectivity, kernel pR and the quotient ring equivalence, and that units have nonzero images.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R004.BG001\", \"R004.BG002\", \"R004.BG003\"]}, {\"id\": \"T020\", \"statement\": \"∀ Q : LR.Residue p K, LR.Bridge p K M Q\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S019\"], \"explanation\": \"Construct Γ and Δ with their specified inverses by tensor/quotient universal properties. ModN is the actual quotient by p multiples with its canonical ZMod action. Check formulas on pure tensors and prove commutation with arbitrary induced lattice and R-linear operators.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R004.BG004\", \"R004.BG005\"]}, {\"id\": \"T021\", \"statement\": \"∀ Q : LR.Residue p K, LR.Transfer p l K M (rho y) v Q\", \"inputs\": [\"T020\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S020\"], \"explanation\": \"For every specified scaled R-basis, base-change to K and transport through Γ. The first-factor convention incorporates the source tensor flips. Preserve the chosen b and basis vectors, and prove the stated K-dimension.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T022\", \"statement\": \"∀ Q : LR.Residue p K, LR.MatrixTransport p l K M rho x y h v Q\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T010\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S021\"], \"explanation\": \"For any witnesses satisfying the exact embedding and Γ formulas, transport integer action matrices and all divided-power matrices. The integer coefficients commute with the ring maps; injectivity of f identifies the required lattice vectors. Construct the reduced operators from actual restrictions.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T023\", \"statement\": \"LR.SmallPowers l K M rho x y h\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T010\", \"T019\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S022\"], \"explanation\": \"Tensor the characteristic-zero identities r!X(r)=x^r and r!Y(r)=y^r. Their restrictions exist by N002; the residue/unit facts make r! nonzero for r≤λ, so cancellation yields the two reduced divided powers.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T024\", \"statement\": \"∀ Q : LR.Residue p K, LR.PolynomialTransport p l K M rho x y h v Q\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T010\", \"T022\", \"T023\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S022\"], \"explanation\": \"Extend the already proved localized matrix-unit identity through Γ, using equality on pure tensors and preservation of compositions. Substitute the small divided-power equalities to obtain the exact polynomial and its coordinate action. All indices λ−i, λ, λ−j satisfy the small-power guard.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T025\", \"statement\": \"LR.N4 p l K M rho x y h v\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T010\", \"T018\", \"T019\", \"T020\", \"T021\", \"T022\", \"T023\", \"T024\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S018\", \"S019\", \"S020\", \"S021\", \"S022\"], \"explanation\": \"Assemble N004: choose residue data, canonical f, coefficient module J and a nonzero cyclic generator from N003, then a scaled R-basis and its transported K-basis. Keep these same witnesses for all matrices and formulas. Export both tensor identifications, the positive dimension, and every reduced matrix-unit clause.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R004.BG001\", \"R004.BG002\", \"R004.BG003\", \"R004.BG004\", \"R004.BG005\"]}, {\"id\": \"T026\", \"statement\": \"∀ (A : LR.Action K M rho x y h) (W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M)), LR.Stable W A.a → LR.Stable W A.d → ∀ i j : LR.I l, LR.Stable W (LR.polynomial l A.a A.d i j)\", \"inputs\": [], \"source_steps\": [\"S023\"], \"explanation\": \"Finite induction proves that invariant subspaces are stable under all nonnegative powers, their compositions and K-scalar multiples. Apply this to the displayed polynomial.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T027\", \"statement\": \"∀ (beta : Module.Basis (LR.I l) K (LR.VK K M)) (W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M)), W ≠ ⊥ → ∃ (w : LR.VK K M) (c : LR.I l → K) (j : LR.I l), w ∈ W ∧ w ≠ 0 ∧ w = ∑ i, c i • beta i ∧ c j ≠ 0\", \"inputs\": [], \"source_steps\": [\"S024\"], \"explanation\": \"Choose a nonzero vector of the nonzero subspace, expand it in the finite basis and select a nonzero coefficient. Close all witnesses inside the existential statement.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R005.BG001\"]}, {\"id\": \"T028\", \"statement\": \"∀ (A : LR.Action K M rho x y h) (beta : Module.Basis (LR.I l) K (LR.VK K M)) (W : Submodule K (LR.VK K M)), (∀ i j k : LR.I l, LR.polynomial l A.a A.d i j (beta k) = if k = j then beta i else 0) → (∀ i j : LR.I l, LR.Stable W (LR.polynomial l A.a A.d i j)) → W ≠ ⊥ → W = ⊤\", \"inputs\": [\"T027\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S025\"], \"explanation\": \"Use the same w,c,j witnesses. Each matrix unit sends w to c_j beta_i; cancel the nonzero scalar in K, then use spanning of the basis.\", \"background_notes\": []}, {\"id\": \"T029\", \"statement\": \"LR.Target K M rho x y h\", \"inputs\": [\"H001\", \"T005\", \"T010\", \"T025\", \"T026\", \"T028\"], \"source_steps\": [\"S026\"], \"explanation\": \"Choose the induced action from N002 and the basis exported by N004. Its positive cardinality proves nontriviality. For every invariant subspace use the zero case or T028. The integer span's scalar extension has the same invariant subspaces as its three generators; no reduced weight separation or algebraic closure is used.\", \"background_notes\": [\"R005.BG002\"]}], \"terminal\": \"T029\"}, \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
