the action matrices.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"I\"]}, {\"symbol\": \"k\", \"type\": \"element of I\", \"scope\": \"R004/argument\", \"start\": \"S004\", \"end\": \"S005\", \"kind\": \"arbitrary\", \"declaration\": \"Fix arbitrary k∈I when evaluating the action matrices.\", \"from_inputs\": [], \"from_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"I\"]}], \"argument\": [{\"id\": \"S001\", \"text\": \"By H001, an integer maps to zero in K exactly when divisible by p. Thus for arbitrary integers A₀,B₀ with B₀≠0 and p∤B₀, the fraction A₀/B₀∈R has a nonzero denominator image in K; and φ(A₀/B₀)=(A₀·1_K)(B₀·1_K)^(-1) is defined. Equality of fractions follows by cross multiplication, so the formula is well-defined and preserves addition, multiplication and 1. The same formula in F_p gives ψ:R→F_p. It is onto, since every integer residue occurs, and its kernel is pR: ψ(A₀/B₀)=0 is equivalent to p∣A₀, hence A₀/B₀=p((A₀/p)/B₀). Conversely multiples of p have zero image. The induced map R/pR→F_p is therefore a bijective ring map. Both constructions agree with the usual inclusion F_p→K. Every unit of R has nonzero image in K, since its image has a multiplicative inverse. In particular N002 shows that r! for 0≤r≤λ and binom(λ,j), j∈I, have nonzero images in K.\", \"uses\": [\"H001\", \"N002\"], \"uses_local\": [], \"objects\": [\"R\", \"p\", \"K\", \"F_p\", \"φ\", \"ψ\", \"λ\", \"I\", \"A₀\", \"B₀\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F001\", \"statement\": \"φ:R→K and ψ:R→F_p are unital ring maps extending the integer maps, ψ is surjective with kernel pR, R/pR≅F_p, and for 0≤r′≤λ and j′∈I the elements r′! and binom(λ,j′) have nonzero images in K.\", \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"r′\", \"type\": \"integer with 0≤r′≤λ\"}, {\"symbol\": \"j′\", \"type\": \"element of I\"}], \"objects\": [\"φ\", \"ψ\", \"R\", \"K\", \"F_p\", \"p\", \"λ\", \"I\", \"r′\", \"j′\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S002\", \"text\": \"Define Γ and Γ' by Γ(m⊗c)=(1⊗m)⊗c and Γ'((r⊗m)⊗c)=m⊗φ(r)c. The defining ℤ-balancing relations in R⊗_ℤ M and in M⊗_ℤ K, and the R-balancing relation in M_R⊗_R K, show that these formulas extend to K-linear maps. They are inverse: Γ'Γ(m⊗c)=m⊗c, and ΓΓ'((r⊗m)⊗c)=(1⊗m)⊗φ(r)c=(r⊗m)⊗c. These tensors generate, so the composites are identities. Next define Δ(z⊗c)=[z]⊗c and Δ'([z]⊗c)=z⊗c. Because p acts as zero on K, changing the lift z by pz' changes the second formula by pz'⊗c=z'⊗pc=0. Using F001, scalar balancing in F_p=R/pR gives the remaining balancing condition; thus both maps are well-defined and K-linear. Their composites are identities on pure tensors. This proves the two scalar-extension isomorphisms with exactly the formulas in the exported conclusion. An operator preserving M extends on all these tensors by applying it to the lattice factor; the displayed formulas commute with that operation. For Δ this also holds for any R-linear operator of M_R, since it preserves pM_R.\", \"uses\": [], \"uses_local\": [\"F001\"], \"objects\": [\"Γ\", \"Γ'\", \"Δ\", \"Δ'\", \"φ\", \"ψ\", \"M\", \"M_R\", \"V_K\", \"R\", \"K\", \"F_p\", \"p\"], \"produces\": [{\"id\": \"F002\", \"statement\": \"Γ and Δ are K-linear isomorphisms with Γ(m′⊗c′)=(1⊗m′)⊗c′, Γ^(-1)((r′⊗m′)⊗c′)=m′⊗φ(r′)c′, Δ(z′⊗c′)=[z′]⊗c′, and Δ^(-1)([z′]⊗c′)=z′⊗c′. They commute with the induced lattice operators (and Δ with all R-linear endomorphisms of M_R).\", \"binders\": [{\"symbol\": \"m′\", \"type\": \"element of M\"}, {\"symbol\": \"c′\", \"type\": \"element of K\"}, {\"symbol\": \"r′\", \"type\": \"element of R\"}, {\"symbol\": \"z′\", \"type\": \"element of M_R\"}], \"objects\": [\"Γ\", \"Δ\", \"φ\", \"M\", \"M_R\", \"V_K\", \"R\", \"K\", \"F_p\", \"m′\", \"c′\", \"r′\", \"z′\"], \"assumptions\": []}], \"introduces\": [], \"discharges\": []}, {\"id\": \"S003\", \"text\": \"Choose b≠0 with J=Rb from N003. That input proves that (b e_i)_(i∈I), regarded in M_R via the injective map ι_M of N002, is an R-basis. Apply M002 with R₀=R, S