s/3/statement\", \"reason\": \"N001 explicitly states |I|=n.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/1/definition\", \"reason\": \"I is the finite integer interval 0≤i≤λ.\"}], \"justification\": \"The checked Fintype.card_eq identifies equality of finite cardinalities with existence of an equivalence. Apply its forward direction to the index type of B and I using the two displayed cardinality equalities. This verifies the routine finite relabeling already performed in S001, rather than assuming F001 as its own premise.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"Fintype.card_eq\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.Data.Fintype.EquivFin\", \"signature\": \"@Fintype.card_eq : ∀ {α : Type u_4} {β : Type u_5} [_F : Fintype.{u_4} α] [_G : Fintype.{u_5} β],\\n  Eq.{1} (Fintype.card.{u_4} α) (Fintype.card.{u_5} β) ↔\\n    Nonempty.{max (u_5 + 1) (u_4 + 1)} (Equiv.{u_4 + 1, u_5 + 1} α β)\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/Data/Fintype/EquivFin.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"5a70d92ff1637a57053c8ccddd92752adb7f0938e9fd585b3cd77badee2e248b\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/402ef25c556ebaee50299bbf3b9cf09cf30d1a19c01e6e6f4a72e3fd196209a5-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"402ef25c556ebaee50299bbf3b9cf09cf30d1a19c01e6e6f4a72e3fd196209a5\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG004\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"Reindexing the integer basis B and the complex basis with the same vectors along the same bijection index(B)≃I yields one family m′:I→M that remains both bases.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inputs/2/statement\", \"reason\": \"H004 gives both basis properties for the same vectors.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"S001 establishes equal cardinalities and reindexes B by I; BG003 verifies the bijection.\"}], \"justification\": \"For the bijection supplied by BG003, checked Module.Basis.reindex_apply gives vector i as B(e.symm i). Apply once over ℤ to M and once over ℂ to V. These vectors agree under M⊂V, so both reindexed bases give the single family asserted in F001. All ring/module premises hold by H004 and the given vector-space structures. This is routine relabeling of the supplied witness.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"Module.Basis.reindex_apply\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs\", \"signature\": \"@Module.Basis.reindex_apply : ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {ι' : Type u_2} {R : Type u_3} {M : Type u_6} [inst : Semiring.{u_3} R]\\n  [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_6} M] [inst_2 : _root_.Module.{u_3, u_6} R M] (b : Module.Basis.{u_1, u_3, u_6} ι R M)\\n  (e : Equiv.{u_1 + 1, u_2 + 1} ι ι') (i' : ι'),\\n  Eq.{u_6 + 1} ((Module.Basis.reindex.{u_1, u_2, u_3, u_6} b e) i') (b ((Equiv.symm.{u_1 + 1, u_2 + 1} e) i'))\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Basis/Defs.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"3cceec3b7ad802f80a65cf4a226522ef1107fbb57ab4e35b830c95d408e7adb3\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/c3c618c4de3c30b823d328fe588e932f1509e8a3596e54658a8e29ee1e0777c6-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"c3c618c4de3c30b823d328fe588e932f1509e8a3596e54658a8e29ee1e0777c6\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG005\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"An additive map in each variable of two ℤ-modules, satisfying integer balancing, extends to an additive map on their tensor product, with the stated values on pure tensors. This applies to D009's multiplication rule and, successively in each pair of factors, to S002's displayed action rule.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/parameters\", \"reason\": \"M is an additive subgroup of the complex vector space V; K is a field.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/6/definition\", \"reason\": \"R embeds in ℚ⊂ℂ; the multiplication rule (r,m)↦rm is additive and integer-balanced.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/8/definition\", \"reason\": \"D009 explicitly defines ι_M by that multiplication rule on pure tensors.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"Before descending the action S002 proves that the integer Chevalley span preserves M, then checks additivity and both integer balancing relations for (t⊗c)(z⊗d)=tz⊗cd.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/1/statement\", \"reason\": \"H002 and the complex module structure give linearity of the original action.\"}], \"justification\": \"The checked TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul applies with base ℤ, additive commutative groups as its two modules and target additive group V or M⊗ℤK. The scalar multiplication map giving ι_M satisfies (ar)m=r(am); its extension is R-linear by multiplication associativity. For the action, t,z↦tz is ℤ-bilinear and K multiplication is additive, so moving an integer between t and c, or between z and d, gives the identical tz⊗cd by the tensor balancing law. Successive lifts therefore implement exactly the author's rule. Multiplying either c or d by a scalar a∈K multiplies cd by a, proving the asserted K-bilinearity. This verifies only the basic tensor descent used in the displayed construction, not a new Lie representation theorem.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basic\", \"signature\": \"@TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul : ∀ {R : Type u_1} [inst : CommSemiring.{u_1} R] {M : Type u_7} {N : Type u_8} {P : Type u_9}\\n  [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_7} M] [inst_2 : AddCommMonoid.{u_8} N] [inst_3 : AddCommMonoid.{u_9} P]\\n  [inst_4 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_7} R M] [inst_5 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_8} R N]\\n  (f : AddMonoidHom.{u_7, max u_9 u_8} M (AddMonoidHom.{u_8, u_9} N P))\\n  (hf :\\n    ∀ (r : R) (m : M) (n : N),\\n      Eq.{u_9 + 1} ((f (HSMul.hSMul.{u_1, u_7, u_7} r m)) n) ((f m) (HSMul.hSMul.{u_1, u_8, u_8} r n)))\\n  (m : M) (n : N),\\n  Eq.{u_9 + 1} ((TensorProduct.liftAddHom.{u_1, u_7, u_8, u_9} f hf) (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R m n))\\n    ((f m) n)\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Basic.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"1559ba16bff8a7deb88b310c3a12220577f1c7b821d8db1beb5e183084e438ec\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/755a2b24ab28d333ee238a8e0fbb40bbc45c400f8e5a6710cc89ccfb8610405b-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"755a2b24ab28d333ee238a8e0fbb40bbc45c400f8e5a6710cc89ccfb8610405b\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG006\", \"at\": \"/infere…5850 tokens truncated…gument/0/produces/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"F001 proves that z↦c_i(z) and c↦c e_i are R-linear, with values in J and M_R.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/3/text\", \"reason\": \"S004 specifies the two balanced formulas and the finite sum over I.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/1/statement\", \"reason\": \"N001 gives finiteness of I.\"}], \"justification\": \"Use the checked lift_tmul with scalar algebra ℚ, first factor ℚ, second factor M_R and target U^I for Θ; for the coordinate maps defining Ψ use second factor J and target T. The curried formulas are ℚ-linear in the first entry and R-linear in the second by the linear coordinate maps and tensor bilinearity checked above. All scalar towers are inherited from R⊂ℚ and scalar extension. This admits only the basic extension operation. The two inverse identities, including their coordinate calculation, remain the author's displayed proof in S004.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lift_tmul\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Tower\", \"signature\": \"@TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lift_tmul : ∀ {R : Type uR} {A : Type uA} {M : Type uM} {N : Type uN} {P : Type uP} [inst : CommSemiring.{uR} R]\\n  [inst_1 : Semiring.{uA} A] [inst_2 : Algebra.{uR, uA} R A] [inst_3 : AddCommMonoid.{uM} M]\\n  [inst_4 : _root_.Module.{uR, uM} R M] [inst_5 : _root_.Module.{uA, uM} A M]\\n  [inst_6 : IsScalarTower.{uR, uA, uM} R A M] [inst_7 : AddCommMonoid.{uN} N] [inst_8 : _root_.Module.{uR, uN} R N]\\n  [inst_9 : AddCommMonoid.{uP} P] [inst_10 : _root_.Module.{uR, uP} R P] [inst_11 : _root_.Module.{uA, uP} A P]\\n  [inst_12 : IsScalarTower.{uR, uA, uP} R A P]\\n  (f : LinearMap.{uA, uA, uM, max uP uN} (RingHom.id.{uA} A) M (LinearMap.{uR, uR, uN, uP} (RingHom.id.{uR} R) N P))\\n  (x : M) (y : N),\\n  Eq.{uP + 1} ((TensorProduct.AlgebraTensorModule.lift.{uR, uA, uM, uN, uP} f) (TensorProduct.tmul.{uR, uM, uN} R x y))\\n    ((f x) y)\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Tower.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"96a469f4bc973fec660d4aba383339abe304d88286e402ade1ca0bd78014c005\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/8bf9431a88f8c19a21795762ad6842bb34c279be2efb9d2206a1c9aa6451163d-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"8bf9431a88f8c19a21795762ad6842bb34c279be2efb9d2206a1c9aa6451163d\"}]}}], \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
{"i":7,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"background-supplement.json\", \"pointer\": \"/notes/R004\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"For every integer A₀, its image in K is zero if and only if (p:ℤ) divides A₀. Thus every denominator B₀ allowed in D007 has nonzero image in K.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/parameters/1\", \"reason\": \"K is a field, hence an additive group with one.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"H001 explicitly supplies characteristic p.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/5/definition\", \"reason\": \"The allowed representatives of R have integer denominators B₀≠0 with p∤B₀.\"}], \"justification\": \"The successfully probed CharP.intCast_eq_zero_iff applies with R=K, the packet's p, and arbitrary integer A₀ (or B₀). All of its structure and characteristic premises are available. This checks the routine characteristic conversion at the start of S001; it supplies no localization map or new hypothesis.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"CharP.intCast_eq_zero_iff\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.Algebra.CharP.Defs\", \"signature\": \"@CharP.intCast_eq_zero_iff : ∀ (R : Type u_1) [inst : AddGroupWithOne.{u_1} R] (p : ℕ) [CharP.{u_1} R p] (a : ℤ),\\n  Eq.{u_1 + 1} (↑a) 0 ↔ Dvd.dvd.{0} (↑p) a\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/Algebra/CharP/Defs.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"c953ac8b9771faa17533f1511b69610f4d217549e5f6a046b01611384b2c51e0\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/bfa936df3a88352bf284ae5051a4d79ceea6d994c4ae7c48bdaaf450d804d7ca-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"bfa936df3a88352bf284ae5051a4d79ceea6d994c4ae7c48bdaaf450d804d7ca\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG002\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"For every integer A₀, [A₀]=0 in F_p=ℤ/pℤ if and only if (p:ℤ) divides A₀. In particular allowed denominators B₀ have nonzero residue.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/15/definition\", \"reason\": \"D017 identifies F_p with ℤ/pℤ and its integer residue map.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/5/definition\", \"reason\": \"D007 requires p∤B₀ for a representative denominator.\"}], \"justification\": \"ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd was successfully probed and its source read at Mathlib/Data/ZMod/Basic.lean:512–513. Instantiate a=A₀ (and then B₀), b=p. It has no primality or extra ring hypothesis. This supplies the basic residue-zero test used in S001, while S001 itself proves the kernel pR using the displayed numerator factorization.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.Data.ZMod.Basic\", \"signature\": \"@ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd : ∀ (a : ℤ) (b : ℕ), Eq.{1} (↑a) 0 ↔ Dvd.dvd.{0} (↑b) a\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/Data/ZMod/Basic.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"9a57047615cf4231f3561aef5c75ededf5a6ed6559308f31e241d1d27b2bc00c\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/1a1a7c0992130a99b1b65c815d497f0c36df1b799c2df6474cd41d30cb3c9131-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"1a1a7c0992130a99b1b65c815d497f0c36df1b799c2df6474cd41d30cb3c9131\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG003\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"For every nonzero u in F_p, u·u^(-1)=1. Consequently the nonzero residue of an allowed denominator B₀ is invertible, as used in constructing ψ.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inputs/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"H001 gives p prime.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/15\", \"reason\": \"D017 defines F_p=ℤ/pℤ as the prime field, guarded by H001.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"The denominator condition p∤B₀ gives [B₀]≠0 by the residue-zero test checked in BG002.\"}], \"justification\": \"The actual local structure condition was checked in the logged read of Mathlib/Algebra/Field/ZMod.lean:17–36: the Field (ZMod p) construction requires exactly Fact p.Prime and proves its inverse cancellation using mul_inv_cancel_aux. The attempted probe of ZMod.instField was rejected because that structure declaration is not a theorem; it is not claimed as a successful source probe. The successfully probed theorem mul_inv_cancel₀ applies to the resulting group-with-zero structure on F_p and u≠0. This is routine field arithmetic for the explicitly defined prime field; it neither proves the core reduction isomorphism nor assumes any project result.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"mul_inv_cancel₀\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Defs\", \"signature\": \"@mul_inv_cancel₀ : ∀ {G₀ : Type u} [inst : GroupWithZero.{u} G₀] {a : G₀},\\n  Ne.{u + 1} a 0 → Eq.{u + 1} (HMul.hMul.{u, u, u} a (Inv.inv.{u} a)) 1\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/Algebra/GroupWithZero/Defs.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"ebd4c4b8ed5e63d9ab41920c182f9c3dde99f8c979b4da118544c73f22b30cc5\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/011f473da50ea416b040b3f8b4179732b0199ab6755abc5aeed6c9158e432199-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"011f473da50ea416b040b3f8b4179732b0199ab6755abc5aeed6c9158e432199\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG004\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"A map additive in each argument and balanced over a commutative semiring extends to an additive map on the tensor product, taking m⊗n to the specified value. This basic construction underlies the displayed Γ, Γ′, Δ, Δ′ formulas and tensor flip.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/6\", \"reason\": \"M_R is R⊗_ℤ M.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/12\", \"reason\": \"V_K is M⊗_ℤ K with its second-factor K action.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"F001 supplies compatible unital ring maps φ and ψ and identifies R/pR with F_p.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"S002 displays the maps, checks the relevant integer and R balancing, and checks representative independence modulo pM_R.\"}], \"justification\": \"TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul was successfully probed; its construction and theorem were read at Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Basic.lean:51–83. Instantiate the base successively as ℤ, R, and F_p with the packet's additive modules. For Γ, integer balancing follows from n(1⊗m)=1⊗nm; for Γ′, inner balancing follows from φ(nr)=nφ(r) and outer balancing from φ(sr)c=φ(r)φ(s)c. For Δ and Δ′, [sz]=ψ(s)[z] and φ(s) is the image of ψ(s); a change z→z+pz′ contributes z′⊗pc=0. The scalar maps agree on fractions by their displayed formulas. Additivity follows from addition in the tensor factors. K-linearity is checked by multiplying the K argument, and the flip uses commutativity of scalar multiplication. This records only the routine tensor extension mechanism; the isomorphisms, inverse calculations and intertwining remain the packet's explicit local proof.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basic\", \"signature\": \"@TensorProduct.liftAddHom_tmul : ∀ {R : Type u_1} [inst : CommSemiring.{u_1} R] {M : Type u_7} {N : Type u_8} {P : Type u_9}\\n  [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_7} M] [inst_2 : AddCommMonoid.{u_8} N] [inst_3 : AddCommMonoid.{u_9} P]\\n  [inst_4 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_7} R M] [inst_5 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_8} R N]\\n  (f : AddMonoidHom.{u_7, max u_9 u_8} M (AddMonoidHom.{u_8, u_9} N P))\\n  (hf :\\n    ∀ (r : R) (m : M) (n : N),\\n      Eq.{u_9 + 1} ((f (HSMul.hSMul.{u_1, u_7, u_7} r m)) n) ((f m) (HSMul.hSMul.{u_1, u_8, u_8} r n)))\\n  (m : M) (n : N),\\n  Eq.{u_9 + 1} ((TensorProduct.liftAddHom.{u_1, u_7, u_8, u_9} f hf) (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R m n))\\n    ((f m) n)\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Basic.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"1559ba16bff8a7deb88b310c3a12220577f1c7b821d8db1beb5e183084e438ec\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/83212450057a0b96c46b90e4d3bcc7e25cbe5e747977154949783fcbb76394a0-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"83212450057a0b96c46b90e4d3bcc7e25cbe5e747977154949783fcbb76394a0\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG005\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"Two linear maps out of a tensor product are equal if they agree on every pure tensor. This applies iteratively to the nested tensor products in ΓΓ′ and Γ′Γ, and to the pure-tensor checks of Δ, Δ′, the tensor flip, and transported operator compositions.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/6\", \"reason\": \"The inner lattice scalar extension is the tensor product R⊗_ℤ M.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/12\", \"reason\": \"V_K is the tensor product M⊗_ℤ K.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"reason\": \"S002 establishes well-defined additive, K-linear maps and displays their composite values on pure tensors.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement\", \"reason\": \"F001 supplies the R and F_p actions on K used in the outer tensor products.\"}], \"justification\": \"TensorProduct.ext′ was successfully probed with its full semilinear signature. In each instance take the identity homomorphism for linear maps, restricting scalars to ℤ, R, or F_p when needed. These are commutative semirings; all factors and codomains are the stated additive modules. Agreement on pure tensors is actually computed in S002. For a nested tensor, apply ext′ to the outer product and then the inner product, equivalently using additivity. The same basic fact validates the explicitly evaluated flip composites in S003 and equality of scalar-extended operators in S005. No associativity, reduction-isomorphism, flatness, or basis theorem is imported by this note.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"TensorProduct.ext'\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Basic\", \"signature\": \"@TensorProduct.ext' : ∀ {R : Type u_1} {R₂ : Type u_2} [inst : CommSemiring.{u_1} R] [inst_1 : CommSemiring.{u_2} R₂]\\n  {σ₁₂ : RingHom.{u_1, u_2} R R₂} {M : Type u_7} {N : Type u_8} {P₂ : Type u_17} [inst_2 : AddCommMonoid.{u_7} M]\\n  [inst_3 : AddCommMonoid.{u_8} N] [inst_4 : AddCommMonoid.{u_17} P₂] [inst_5 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_7} R M]\\n  [inst_6 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_8} R N] [inst_7 : _root_.Module.{u_2, u_17} R₂ P₂]\\n  {g h : LinearMap.{u_1, u_2, max u_8 u_7, u_17} σ₁₂ (TensorProduct.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R M N) P₂},\\n  (∀ (x : M) (y : N),\\n      Eq.{u_17 + 1} (g (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R x y)) (h (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R x y))) →\\n    Eq.{max (max (u_17 + 1) (u_7 + 1)) (u_8 + 1)} g h\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Basic.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"1559ba16bff8a7deb88b310c3a12220577f1c7b821d8db1beb5e183084e438ec\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/e692715fe3d16dd6cab6c6801f3e87efa13b919ca3301040fc37888ac33b203d-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"e692715fe3d16dd6cab6c6801f3e87efa13b919ca3301040fc37888ac33b203d\"}]}}], \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}
{"i":8,"result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":"{\"id\": \"background-supplement.json\", \"pointer\": \"/notes/R005\", \"view\": \"value\", \"value\": [{\"id\": \"BG001\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/1/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"For the chosen finite K-basis β:I→V_K, every u∈V_K satisfies u=Σ_(i∈I)(β.repr(u))_i β_i. This supplies the expansion in S002 and, once every β_i lies in W, the conclusion W=V_K in S003.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/inputs/3/statement\", \"reason\": \"N004 explicitly supplies the K-basis β of V_K.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/1/definition\", \"reason\": \"I is the finite integer interval 0≤i≤λ.\"}, {\"location\": \"/parameters/1/type\", \"reason\": \"K is a field, hence a semiring; V_K is its module.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"The basis is legitimately chosen before S002 and W is a K-linear subspace.\"}], \"justification\": \"The probed Module.Basis.sum_repr has precisely the finite-index, semiring, additive-commutative-monoid and module premises supplied here. Instantiate its scalar ring by K, module by V_K, index type by I and basis by β. In S002 W≠{0} plus 0∈W already gives a nonzero w by set extensionality; this note supplies only the routine expansion, not witness existence. If all coefficients vanish the displayed finite sum vanishes, so a coefficient is nonzero. In S003 subspace closure under the same finite sum yields membership for every u. No new basis or project theorem is assumed.\", \"hint\": \"Use Module.Basis.sum_repr with a Fintype for the finite interval I.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"Module.Basis.sum_repr\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Basis.Defs\", \"signature\": \"@Module.Basis.sum_repr : ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {R : Type u_3} {M : Type u_6} [inst : Semiring.{u_3} R] [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_6} M]\\n  [inst_2 : _root_.Module.{u_3, u_6} R M] [inst_3 : Fintype.{u_1} ι] (b : Module.Basis.{u_1, u_3, u_6} ι R M) (u : M),\\n  Eq.{u_6 + 1} (∑ i, HSMul.hSMul.{u_3, u_6, u_6} (((Module.Basis.repr.{u_1, u_3, u_6} b) u) i) (b i)) u\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/Basis/Defs.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"3cceec3b7ad802f80a65cf4a226522ef1107fbb57ab4e35b830c95d408e7adb3\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/b95bd4b78849092e5afed05dbe2eb71438266130c1732147f7ac56ff9a83b27e-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"b95bd4b78849092e5afed05dbe2eb71438266130c1732147f7ac56ff9a83b27e\"}]}}, {\"id\": \"BG002\", \"at\": \"/inference/argument/3/text\", \"kind\": \"mathlib\", \"fact\": \"With A=ℤx+ℤy+ℤh, the three elements x⊗1,y⊗1,h⊗1 span A⊗_ℤ K=L_K over K. Hence a K-linear subspace of V_K invariant under their ρ_K-actions is invariant under every L_K-action, and conversely.\", \"grounds\": [{\"location\": \"/definitions/13/definition\", \"reason\": \"D015 defines L_K as the scalar extension of the integer span of these three elements.\"}, {\"location\": \"/definitions/14/definition\", \"reason\": \"D016 defines the action K-linearly, with invariance conventions for the three generators.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inputs/2/statement\", \"reason\": \"N002 provides closure of the integer span and well-definedness and K-linearity of its scalar-extended Lie action.\"}, {\"location\": \"/parameters/1/type\", \"reason\": \"K is a field and thus an additive commutative group and canonical ℤ-module.\"}, {\"location\": \"/inference/argument/0/text\", \"reason\": \"Under L1 W is a K-linear subspace invariant under each of the three actions.\"}], \"justification\": \"Apply the probed TensorProduct.induction_on at R=ℤ, M=A, N=K to membership in the K-span of the three displayed tensors. All additive commutative monoid and ℤ-module premises hold because A is an additive integer span and K is a field. The zero and addition cases follow from subspace axioms. For a pure tensor u⊗c, write u=m x+n y+q h with m,n,q∈ℤ by the definition of A; the defining bilinear and balanced tensor relations give u⊗c=(m·1_K)c (x⊗1)+(n·1_K)c (y⊗1)+(q·1_K)c (h⊗1). Each term is in that span. This is the routine justification of the existing generator-spanning sentence, not a new irreducibility route. K-linearity of ρ_K and subspace closure then give invariance under any such linear combination; the reverse implication is restriction to the three elements.\", \"source\": {\"declaration\": \"TensorProduct.induction_on\", \"module\": \"Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.TensorProduct.Defs\", \"signature\": \"@TensorProduct.induction_on : ∀ {R : Type u_1} [inst : CommSemiring.{u_1} R] {M : Type u_7} {N : Type u_8} [inst_1 : AddCommMonoid.{u_7} M]\\n  [inst_2 : AddCommMonoid.{u_8} N] [inst_3 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_7} R M] [inst_4 : _root_.Module.{u_1, u_8} R N]\\n  {motive : TensorProduct.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R M N → Prop} (z : TensorProduct.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R M N),\\n  motive 0 →\\n    (∀ (x : M) (y : N), motive (TensorProduct.tmul.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R x y)) →\\n      (∀ (x y : TensorProduct.{u_1, u_7, u_8} R M N),\\n          motive x → motive y → motive (HAdd.hAdd.{max u_7 u_8, max u_7 u_8, max u_7 u_8} x y)) →\\n        motive z\", \"declaration_kind\": \"theorem\", \"source_path\": \"Mathlib/LinearAlgebra/TensorProduct/Defs.lean\", \"source_sha256\": \"9dde2d95f3492e97f3b4f02e49dff3b08770f2e17556853c80e974b17e5d08c9\", \"mathlib_commit\": \"905b95818eb32af7874a58b427f50c1711a5e96c\", \"evidence\": [{\"path\": \"review-evidence/reuse/24d82c380d3f818752d35bafc6ee56568a8d63c47a16699b88abec37ba0050ac-library-sources.json\", \"sha256\": \"24d82c380d3f818752d35bafc6ee56568a8d63c47a16699b88abec37ba0050ac\"}]}}], \"has_more\": false, \"next_offset\": null}"}}

